A||N||1||NoWing87||New member- Multiple leaks||||1||
Z||000000||NoWing87||11-25-2000||10:02 AM||||Two questions...I have an 87GT with several oil leaks on the bottom side. I have not dropped the oil pan yet.<BR>1. My Hayes manual says the rear main oil seal is two piece an can be changed with motor in place. But I went to parts store an they pulled out a one piece, therefore no in-car fix, right?<BR>2. Also have leak around right axle shaft coming out of the transaxle. Can this seal be replaced from the outside? (Part store had no listing) Is it true the transaxle uses 5W30 instead of gear oil(80-90)?<P>Thanks, NoWing87||||reg||1
Z||000001||StuGood||11-25-2000||11:58 AM||||Good thing you haven't dropped the oil pan yet!  Might dent it, and would hurt if you dropped it on your foot  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Har, har  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif">, right?  Okay-<BR>1)Before you decide it's the rear main seal, see if it's just the distributor o-ring that needs replacing.  Haynes and Chilton's manuals talk about a one-piece rear main seal that can be replace w/o removing the pan.<P>2)Transaxle driveshaft seal can be replaced  from outside.  You have to take the axle out, though.  Replacement seal is available almost anywhere (AutoZone, NAPA, etc.).  Think it's a very common seal for GM FWD cars.  I use a piece of PVC pipe (2-1/2"?) to drive the seal into Xaxle.<P>Yes, the 1987 owner's manual says use 5W-30. <B>Not</B> gear oil.  Later, GM changed their recommendation to "GM Synchromesh Fluid" for the 5-speed Getrag transaxles.  It's expensive, but it's what I use.  Cheap insurance.||||reg||12
